 "parts": [
  "What do the letters O and K stand for?",
  "The best documented answer is a joke from Boston.",
  "In the late eighteen-thirties, newspaper writers misspelled phrases on purpose.",
  "Then they shortened them to initials.",
  "K.G. meant know go.",
  "On March twenty-third, eighteen thirty-nine, the Boston Morning Post printed O.K.",
  "It stood for oll korrect. A joke spelling of all correct.",
  "That is the earliest printed example anyone has found.",
  "Professor Allen Walker Read dug it up more than a century later.",
  "Most of those silly abbreviations vanished.",
  "OK survived because of an election.",
  "Martin Van Buren, nickname Old Kinderhook, ran again in eighteen forty.",
  "His supporters formed OK Clubs.",
  "But this is the best documented explanation. Not the only one.",
  "Some point to a Choctaw word. Others, to West Africa.",
  "Scholars did not find a clear trail.",
  "Almost two hundred years old, and you probably used it today."
 ],
